{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_09487140ad67cf600169bb41f9c4248191956181f416ff4e1b", "created": 1773879801, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Cancer Survivorship Follow-Up Care: Visits, Tests, Lung Nodules, and Support Resources\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on post-treatment and post-finding surveillance activities—particularly FOLLOW-UP CARE and FOLLOW-UP VISITS—linking clinical monitoring, testing, and survivorship support needs. Key components include structured follow-up appointments (especially for gliomas due to recurrence risk), targeted surveillance for LUNG NODULE subtypes (SOLID and SUBSOLID), annual FULL SKIN EXAMS overseen by a DOCTOR, and planning for SCREENING FOR LATE EFFECTS. Practical coordination elements such as SCHEDULING and access concerns (including affordability) appear as cross-cutting themes, alongside patient interest in a survivor SUPPORT GROUP resource. Medical testing plays a dual role in PLANNING TREATMENT and CHECKING TREATMENT EFFECTIVENESS, connecting diagnostic/monitoring workflows to care decisions. [Data: Entities (60, 4866, 1011, 19, 6407); Relationships (12253, 1570, 1551, 1552, 14588, +more)]\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"FOLLOW-UP CARE functions as the hub connecting survivorship monitoring, late-effect surveillance, and nodule follow-up pathways\",\n      \"explanation\": \"FOLLOW-UP CARE is described as a planned, ongoing monitoring and management process after major health events (e.g., cancer treatment completion) or abnormal findings (e.g., nodule detection). It explicitly spans coordinated clinical assessments and appropriate testing to detect recurrence/progression/new problems and support long-term health. [Data: Entities (60)]\\n\\nWithin the relationship network, FOLLOW-UP CARE is directly tied to both lung nodule subtype surveillance (SOLID NODULES) and survivorship plans for SCREENING FOR LATE EFFECTS and LATE EFFECTS. This positions FOLLOW-UP CARE as the central organizing concept that bridges disease surveillance (nodules), survivorship complications (late effects), and longitudinal planning. [Data: Relationships (1570, 14629, 14633); Entities (60, 1038, 6388, 6405)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"FOLLOW-UP VISITS are framed as a long-term surveillance program, strongly motivated by glioma recurrence risk and requiring sustained coordination\",\n      \"explanation\": \"FOLLOW-UP VISITS are characterized as periodic, post-treatment monitoring appointments recommended because gliomas often recur and may require additional rounds of treatment. The description emphasizes a structured surveillance plan with more frequent visits in the first 1–2 years, tapering through year 5 and beyond, with timing individualized based on recurrence risk, clinical status, and patient preferences. [Data: Entities (4866)]\\n\\nThe relationship between FOLLOW-UP VISITS and RECURRENCE explicitly states that recurrence risk is a key driver for recommending these visits. A separate relationship links FOLLOW-UP VISITS to SCHEDULING, underscoring that long-duration surveillance creates operational demands (who schedules, how often, and over how many years). [Data: Relationships (12253, 14639); Entities (4866, 6402)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"LUNG NODULE management in this community is imaging-led and differentiated by SOLID vs SUBSOLID categories\",\n      \"explanation\": \"LUNG NODULES are described as small lung tissue masses often found incidentally; they are usually not cancer but can be, and are evaluated using imaging, risk assessment, and sometimes biopsy. The relationships reinforce imaging as the primary discovery and evaluation mechanism, including review and potentially additional imaging to estimate cancer likelihood. [Data: Entities (1011); Relationships (1548)]\\n\\nThe nodule domain is further subdivided into SOLID NODULES and SUBSOLID NODULES as categories “requiring specific follow-up approaches.” This implies a structured decision tree in follow-up planning where surveillance intensity and steps depend on nodule type. [Data: Entities (1038, 1039, 1011); Relationships (1551, 1552)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Survivorship planning explicitly includes late-effect identification and ongoing screening strategies\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes both LATE EFFECTS (long-term effects appearing or persisting after treatment) and SCREENING FOR LATE EFFECTS (monitoring/testing plans for long-term treatment effects). These concepts indicate that survivorship is not limited to recurrence surveillance; it also includes anticipating and monitoring treatment sequelae over time. [Data: Entities (6405, 6388)]\\n\\nFOLLOW-UP CARE is directly connected to both SCREENING FOR LATE EFFECTS and LATE EFFECTS in relationships that describe survivorship follow-up as including screening and planning for how late effects will be monitored. This establishes late-effect screening as a formal component of follow-up care planning, not an ad hoc concern. [Data: Relationships (14629, 14633); Entities (60, 6388, 6405)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"FULL SKIN EXAMS appear as a defined preventive surveillance activity within follow-up, with physician oversight emphasized\",\n      \"explanation\": \"FULL SKIN EXAMS are defined as ongoing follow-up evaluations performed by a doctor at least annually after treatment to monitor for recurrence or new skin cancers. This inserts a concrete, recurring preventive screening task into the broader follow-up landscape. [Data: Entities (19)]\\n\\nA relationship explicitly links FULL SKIN EXAMS to DOCTOR, stating that a doctor performs or oversees the exams during follow-up care. This reinforces clinical accountability and suggests that follow-up care includes both scheduled surveillance actions and defined responsible parties. [Data: Relationships (47); Entities (19)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"MEDICAL TESTS connect decision-making (planning) with longitudinal assessment (effectiveness), indicating a feedback loop in care\",\n      \"explanation\": \"MEDICAL TESTS are described as being used both to plan treatment (including biopsy-based analyses and biomarker/genetic testing) and to check how well treatment is working, implying repeated measurement and adaptation across the care continuum. [Data: Entities (5257)]\\n\\nTwo explicit relationships tie MEDICAL TESTS to PLANNING TREATMENT and CHECKING TREATMENT EFFECTIVENESS, establishing tests as a shared dependency for initial decisions and ongoing evaluation. In the context of this community, that aligns with the broader theme of structured monitoring and reassessment over time. [Data: Relationships (11681, 11682); Entities (5257, 5258, 5259)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Patient support needs and operational barriers (scheduling and affordability) are embedded as practical determinants of follow-up continuity\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A SUPPORT GROUP is included as a survivorship resource sought by the patient, and a relationship states the patient is looking for a survivor support group and asks for recommendations. This indicates psychosocial/support resource navigation is part of the survivorship ecosystem represented in this network. [Data: Entities (6407); Relationships (14588)]\\n\\nOperational feasibility is also present: SCHEDULING is defined around coordinating appointments and tests (including questions about responsibility for scheduling and selecting days/times), and FOLLOW-UP VISITS are explicitly linked to SCHEDULING. Additionally, the FOLLOW-UP CARE description notes practical barriers including addressing financial difficulties related to follow-up visits and tests so recommended monitoring can be completed. Together, these elements show that adherence is influenced by logistics and access, not solely clinical intent. [Data: Entities (6402, 60, 4866); Relationships (14639)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 3.6,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact severity is low-to-moderate because the community reflects consequential healthcare surveillance and support coordination needs (recurrence monitoring, nodule follow-up, late-effect screening) but contains no indications of illicit activity or acute external harm. [Data: Entities (60, 4866, 1011, 6388, 6407); Relationships (12253, 1548, 14629, 14588)]\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1940, "prompt_tokens": 3363, "total_tokens": 5303, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 226, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}